hey there guys do i have an exercise and  a half for you today today we're going  to be talking about the hindu push-up  also called dand and very similar to  another exercise called the dive bomber  so essentially the hindu push-up if  you've not heard of it before it's a  push-up where you're starting in  something akin to a pike push-up  position or even a downward dog and then  you're going to dive down towards the  ground and swoop up at the top this has  so many benefits it's an exercise i  think that everyone should be doing and  yet say a few people do do it why is no  one doing it this is something we need  to put a stop to but never fear the  bioneer is here  but first let me try and sell you on why  you should be doing the hindu push-up  i'll try and convince you of its  benefits this is a single exercise that  can improve your hamstring flexibility  your shoulder strength your core  strength your pecs your triceps even  open up your shoulders and build  coordination it's a fantastic movement  then and because you're getting all this  in one go it means you have to spend  long amounts of time stretching on a mat  somewhere so to begin with you're  starting off in what is essentially a  downward dog position now here there's  no strict requirement to get your heels  flat on the floor to keep your legs nice  and straight however there's no reason  not to aim for that because if you do  then you're getting this fantastic  hamstring stretch this fantastic calf  stretch and so many of us have tight  hamstrings and tight calves which  impedes our movements whether we're  jumping or running which makes us stiff  and achy in the mornings particularly in  the lower back and which just robs us of  energy if you can't easily reach down  and touch the floor or kick above your  head then you're going to find that this  makes you feel generally stiffer you'll  recover more slowly from workouts and  you'll be much more likely to injure  yourself so finding a way to incorporate  this kind of stretch into your routine  without spending ages just in downward  dog doing nothing is a fantastic win at  the same time this is also a brilliant  stretch for your shoulders because in  that position you're going to be trying  to push your head and shoulders through  the arms keeping them nice and straight  and opening up the shoulders that way  improving your overhead mobility  therefore improving things like  handstand push-up military press etc  this is even going to build your  compressive strength as this should be  an active kind of stretch where you're  using your core and your hip flexors to  compress your own body a pike push-up of  course being an exercise that you can  use to build up to the handstand push-up  where you're diving down at a much  steeper angle and thereby putting the  emphasis on your deltoids you're getting  that same starting position with a nice  eccentric contraction thereby  strengthening those muscles as well then  of course you've got the pecs and the  core because you're keeping these all  nice and rigid and the triceps even more  so than regular push-up because your  hands are further back down and then  you're opening up the spine and here  comes that thoracic mobility this spine  mobility which can help you again to  avoid injury and stiffness and which at  the same time can improve lots of  different movements whether it's the  squat and you're trying to keep your  back nice and upright or whether it's an  overhead squat whether it's a backhand  spring or a bridge and at the same time  you've got a little forwards lean there  with straight arms so you're building  the same kind of straight arm strength  and shoulder strength that you would  need for something like the planche all  of this is in a gentle and controlled  way that you can do for relatively high  repetitions without a risk of injury so  what a fantastic exercise the hindu  push-up really is there are a few common  mistakes people make with a hindi  push-up and if you find that this  movement feels awkward or difficult then  you might be making some of them  yourself so i'm going to talk you  through it here whilst highlighting some  issues and things that people normally  get wrong so if you start out in a plank  position this is the best starting  position and you want to stack your  wrists underneath your shoulders that's  where things can often go wrong because  if you're too close forwards or too far  backwards then you're going to have a  weird pike it's going to be too narrow  and that's going to make it difficult  for you to move or it's going to be too  long and you're going to be too  stretched out so if you start from here  you should be in a good position then  you're going to try and put your bum up  as high as you can in the air get your  heels flat onto the ground if you can  keep your legs straight if you can then  you're going to dive through the middle  like so not touching the ground not  resting your waist on the floor so the  whole time you've got a contraction here  looking up with your shoulders and your  head now in front of your hands and your  arms locked out straight and you're  gonna hold this position for a moment  trying to feel the flexion in the spine  just below the shoulder blades if  possible because thoracic extension is  really what we want as opposed to lumbar  but obviously that's going to happen as  well and then you just  bring the butt back up straight back  into that downward dog position and like  i say when you're here try and keep the  back flat try and push your head through  your hands to open up the shoulders  nicely  do it in a controlled motion you also  want to make sure that your hands are  far enough apart to get your body  through  some other variations of the hindu  push-up include the mckenzie up here  what you're going to do is you're going  to lie yourself flat on the ground then  you're going to put your arms by your  side as they were before and then just  going to raise your upper body just  using the strength in your spine to lift  the upper portion of your back as much  as you can not to the point of  discomfort or you feel any kind of  twinging and not pushing off your hands  they're just there for balance you're  just going to use the strength of your  upper back so it's a lot like the  superman in that regard of course don't  let stuart mcgill hear you say any of  this but if you check my video on back  strength you'll know there are multiple  views on just how useful it is to extend  your spine like this  or alternatively if you don't really  want the strength component and you just  want the stretch component then what you  can do is you can break this down into  two different movements the downward dog  and then the cobra pose the downward dog  we've already talked about the cobra  pose is slightly different because what  you're going to do here is going to keep  your feet flat so toes pointing out but  you're still going to keep your hips off  of the ground they're not going to touch  the ground you're not resting at any  point and in fact you're going to push  through the hips in order to get a nice  stretch on and to engage some strength  there as well and you're just going to  hold that position with your back nicely  up again  the dive bomber is a movement where you  get into that position which looks very  much like the cobra and then you simply  reverse that position back up the way  you came so it looks almost as though  you've rewound the movement this  requires more coordination and it's a  little bit harder you've got more of a  push going back the other way so you  might want to build up to that one this  is another benefit of the hindu push-up  it's fantastic for coordination  you have your quadrilateral movements  where you're coordinating the left and  the right side of your body here you're  really coordinating the waist with the  shoulders and the spine all moving in  one fluid motion you've got to be aware  of all those things at once this is the  kind of thing that can translate to  better movement in martial arts in dance  and help you to learn skills more  quickly especially because you're  building that mobility at the same time  it also activates the vestibular system  because your head is swooping like this  it really wakes you up because the  brain's like what's going on there's a  lot of movement here and it needs to  coordinate itself and it needs to figure  out where you are in space it's a great  youtube channel where you can learn more  about this called original strength i  highly recommend checking it out and to  do this your brain is going to use  signals that come from your whole body  but particularly your neck where you  have a particularly high density of  muscle spindles these being sensors that  tell us about movement and about changes  in length and what you're going to do is  use that information coming from your  neck from these muscle spindles to  figure out where your head is in  relation to the rest of your body so  you've got the vestibular system in your  ears shaking around and moving about and  your body would think that you're  falling if it wasn't for the senses the  proprioception it's getting from the  rest of your body and particularly your  neck so it knows where your head is in  space compared with the rest of your  body so learning this is fantastic for  improving like your aerial mobility if  you're doing jumps and things and your  balance in general and a lot of us have  very stiff necks we all know that we  have the henched over texting position  neck so if you're stretching out the  neck and keeping it nice and loose then  again not only is this going to help you  to limber up and avoid stiffness and  injury and pain it's also going to  further improve your balance and your  vestibular control hey guys adam from  the future here and no the months  haven't been kind so another variation  is the hanuman dan or the hanuman hindu  push-up and hanuman being a indian god i  think i'm certainly not the person to be  asking about that but essentially what  we're going to do here is going to bring  one leg up step forwards and through  thereby getting a really nice stretch on  the hip flexors then you perform  push-ups in that position and also arch  and raise the back in a similar manner  so it looks something like this  [Music]  as you can see slightly less of a  sweeping motion but i've seen it done  with more of a sweeping motion and i  think it's really what you find useful  and comfortable  cool thing about this is it might look  familiar if you do animal flow it looks  like the front step and in a similar  manner you can combine it with things  like a kick through or all kinds of  other variations to create this kind of  amazing push-ups flow which is something  that i really like to do  i hope you found this video useful and  interesting guys if you did then please  leave a like and share it around that  helps me out immensely of course if you  want to see more like it then subscribe  and hit the bell button so you get  notifications and let me know in the  comments down below whether you use the  hindu push-up already whether this video  has convinced you to use it now and what  other movements you'd like to see me  highlight on this channel in future if  you want to find more exercises like  this and if you want to train in a way  that does more than just build strength  and aesthetics but also trains mobility  speed reflexes balance even brain  function then you might enjoy my ebook  and training program super functional  training which you can find in the  description down below i also have a  print book out called functional  training and beyond which is an  introduction to functional training as  it applies to the general population and  athletes either way thank you so much  for watching this one guys i appreciate  it so much and i'll see you next time  bye for now  [Music]

